@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 15: tables, and results as tables)

A table holds named columns of different types, like a spreadsheet. The CSV in this document's sidecar folder is invented.

@section(Making and reading a table)

Build one from columns with `table`, or read a file with `readtable`.

@octo
T = readtable("15-tables.assets/scores.csv")
height(T), width(T), T.Properties.VariableNames
summary(T)
@end

@section(Selecting rows and columns)

Dot-names give a column; parentheses with a logical mask give rows.

@octo
T = readtable("15-tables.assets/scores.csv");
T.exam'
T(T.exam > 80, :)
T(:, {"student", "exam"})
sortrows(T, "exam", "descend")(1:2, :)
@end

@section(Changing a table)

Add, rename and remove columns; convert to and from arrays and cells.

@octo
T = readtable("15-tables.assets/scores.csv");
T.total = T.quiz * 2 + T.exam;
T.Properties.VariableNames{3} = "q";
T.section = [];
T(1:2, :)
A = table2array(T(:, {"q", "exam"}));
size(A)
U = array2table([1 2; 3 4], "VariableNames", {"a", "b"})
C = table2cell(U)
S = table2struct(U); S(2).b
@end

@section(Group summaries and matching rows)

`grpstats` summarises by group; `ismember` matches rows of two tables on a key column.

@octo
T = readtable("15-tables.assets/scores.csv");
[mn, grp] = grpstats(T.exam, T.section, {"mean", "gname"})
mean(table2array(T(:, {"quiz", "exam"})))
names = table({"S01"; "S03"; "S05"}, {"Rin"; "Tal"; "Ode"}, "VariableNames", {"student", "name"});
[found, at] = ismember(names.student, T.student);
names.exam = T.exam(at)
@end

@section(Results as tables)

A model's coefficients come back as named columns (Name, Estimate, SE, tStat, pValue) that you index like a table's.

@octo
T = readtable("15-tables.assets/scores.csv");
m = fitlm(T, "exam ~ quiz");
C = m.Coefficients
C.Estimate(2)
C.pValue' < 0.05
@end
